```rust
struct Session;
struct Account {
    balance: i64,
    alerts_sent: usize,
    session: Option<Session>,
}

impl Account {
    // Query name, but sends alerts and counts them
    pub fn balance(&mut self) -> i64 {
        if self.balance < 0 {
            self.alerts_sent += 1;
            send_alert();
        }
        self.balance
    }

    // Password check that silently creates a session
    pub fn check_password(&mut self, pass: &str) -> bool {
        if pass == "secret" {
            self.session = Some(Session);
            true
        } else {
            false
        }
    }
}
```

counterexample:
Lazy caching (memoization) where the mutated field is purely an internal performance optimization and does not change semantic behavior. A read-only query plus a separate explicit command; an explicitly named and documented command such as `login_user` may return a result while performing its advertised state transition:
```rust
pub fn check_password(username: &str, secret: &str) -> bool {
    let user = find_user(username);
    user.verify_pass(secret)
}

pub fn login_user(username: &str, secret: &str) {
    if check_password(username, secret) {
        Session::initialize();
    }
}

impl Account {
    pub fn balance(&self) -> i64 {
        self.balance
    }
    pub fn trigger_overdraft_alerts(&mut self) {
        if self.balance < 0 {
            self.alerts_sent += 1;
            send_alert();
        }
    }
}
```

fix:
Separate read-only checking from explicit mutation where they are independent: keep the query pure and move the state change into a named command. Keep authentication verification and session creation inside an explicit login command that gates session changes on successful verification; do not expose an unconditional session initializer as an equivalent safe login API. Preserve required ordering and consistency when extracting commands, and keep inseparable transitions atomic rather than introducing a check-then-act race.
```rust
// Caller must execute sequentially:
account.trigger_overdraft_alerts();
let bal = account.balance();
```
